two and three.

01:06:50.300 --> 01:06:51.180
Right?

01:06:51.180 --> 01:06:53.400
Do we agree with that?

01:06:53.400 --> 01:06:56.340
But I'm saying with mean
absolute error going from W

01:06:56.340 --> 01:06:58.340
equals four to W equals five
hertz,

01:06:58.340 --> 01:07:01.340
you're getting further away on
average from the data.

01:07:01.340 --> 01:07:04.560
But for mean squared error,
where the goal is to minimize

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:08.040
the average squared distance
from all the data points,

01:07:08.320 --> 01:07:12.240
the fact that you have this one
outlier sort of skews your

01:07:12.240 --> 01:07:15.240
parameters and or predictions
accordingly.

01:07:17.320 --> 01:07:19.100
Do we see why that is?

01:07:19.100 --> 01:07:19.620
OK,

01:07:19.620 --> 01:07:22.000
so now we're looking at this in
the context of the constant

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:23.000
model,

01:07:23.340 --> 01:07:28.720
but the fact that using squared
loss sort of pushes your

01:07:28.720 --> 01:07:31.260
predictions in the direction of
outliers

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:35.740
is true with other models as
well,

01:07:35.740 --> 01:07:37.990
including squared loss.

01:07:38.140 --> 01:07:41.220
And this is actually a lab
question you will see tomorrow.

01:07:41.220 --> 01:07:45.580
There will be a lab question
where there's a data set and two

01:07:45.580 --> 01:07:46.580
lines.

01:07:46.900 --> 01:07:50.300
One will say this is the line
that minimizes mean absolute

01:07:50.300 --> 01:07:51.300
error.

01:07:51.520 --> 01:07:53.620
One minimizes mean squared
error.

01:07:53.620 --> 01:07:55.880
And you have to think about
which one is which.

01:07:55.880 --> 01:07:58.920
And the answer is that the one
that minimizes mean squared

01:07:58.920 --> 01:08:01.920
error is the one that is pulled
more in the direction of the

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:03.280
outliers.
And it's because of this

01:08:03.280 --> 01:08:05.320
behavior.
OK, in general,

01:08:05.760 --> 01:08:09.510
squared loss is more influenced
by outliers.

01:08:37.060 --> 01:08:39.000
Coming back here,

01:08:39.000 --> 01:08:41.480
so squared loss is more
influenced by outliers than

01:08:41.480 --> 01:08:42.980
absolute loss.

01:08:43.400 --> 01:08:46.780
What if instead of squaring the
loss,

01:08:46.780 --> 01:08:51.980
I raised it to the power of 4?

01:08:51.980 --> 01:08:54.800
Well, what would you think would
happen?

01:08:54.800 --> 01:08:55.180
Alex?

01:08:55.180 --> 01:08:56.300
It would be the same effect,

01:08:56.300 --> 01:08:57.880
but even way more extreme.

01:08:57.880 --> 01:08:58.680
Exactly.

01:08:58.680 --> 01:09:03.220
So now your distance from the
outliers is being penalized

01:09:03.220 --> 01:09:05.940
quartically, which is the term
for degree four,

01:09:05.940 --> 01:09:08.190
rather than quadratically.

01:09:08.840 --> 01:09:11.760
What that means is your penalty
from being far away from an

01:09:11.760 --> 01:09:14.020
outlier increases even more.
And so you can imagine the

01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:16.420
outlier has even more of a pull.

01:09:16.420 --> 01:09:19.460
What if you did degree 100 loss?

01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:20.960
Then what?

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:28.700
I'm sort of baiting you into
predicting what the answer is

01:09:28.700 --> 01:09:30.950
going to be.

01:09:32.980 --> 01:09:34.820
Your guess is okay,

01:09:34.820 --> 01:09:37.180
degree two you get pulled by the
outlier, degree four you get

01:09:37.180 --> 01:09:38.260
pulled even more.

01:09:38.260 --> 01:09:40.220
So maybe the picture you have in
your mind is degree four brings

01:09:40.220 --> 01:09:41.120
you here,

01:09:41.120 --> 01:09:43.520
and degree a million brings you
here.

01:09:43.520 --> 01:09:45.920
That was my initial prediction
when I first started researching

01:09:45.920 --> 01:09:46.540
this,

01:09:46.540 --> 01:09:48.600
but that's not what happens.

01:09:48.600 --> 01:09:50.880
Pretend you have degree a
million loss.

01:09:50.880 --> 01:09:52.660
The optimal W isn't this,

01:09:52.660 --> 01:09:57.910
but what do you think it is?

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:08.760
It's not the median, it's the
midpoint.

01:10:08.760 --> 01:10:11.760
It's halfway between the
smallest and biggest data point.

01:10:12.160 --> 01:10:16.500
Because if you think about what
degree 100 loss would be,

01:10:16.500 --> 01:10:19.620
then your penalty for being far
away from anything is very high.

01:10:19.620 --> 01:10:22.040
So if you overcorrect it and
say, OK, I'm just going to live

01:10:22.040 --> 01:10:23.820
on w equals 14,

01:10:23.820 --> 01:10:26.440
well, then think about how big
your loss to the data point one

01:10:26.440 --> 01:10:27.620
would be.

01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:30.460
So as that exponent gets bigger
and bigger,

01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:34.320
the optimal parameter converges
to halfway between the min and

01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:37.520
the max so that its worst case
distance to any point is

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:38.380
minimized.

01:10:38.380 --> 01:10:40.920
Because if you're halfway
between the min and the max,

01:10:40.920 --> 01:10:44.460
then that is the smallest worst
case distance you could have.

01:10:44.460 --> 01:10:47.940
Because if you move away from
that, then the furthest you are

01:10:47.940 --> 01:10:49.820
away from any data point
increases.

01:10:49.820 --> 01:10:51.180
But if you're in the middle,

01:10:51.180 --> 01:10:54.180
then while it's not necessarily
indicative of the data set,

01:10:54.180 --> 01:10:57.900
you're not that far from the
smallest or the biggest.

01:10:57.900 --> 01:11:01.100
But this is like a sort of
confusing idea, not something

01:11:01.100 --> 01:11:05.200
that I've seen published or
anything like that anywhere.

01:11:05.220 --> 01:11:07.680
This is more of like a research
idea, but it's like the kind of

01:11:07.680 --> 01:11:10.320
thing you'll learn how to play
around with in lab tomorrow.

01:11:10.320 --> 01:11:11.500
There is a question.

01:11:11.500 --> 01:11:12.240
Sam, yeah.

01:11:12.240 --> 01:11:14.180
Wait, would that be the,

01:11:14.180 --> 01:11:16.020
are you saying that's the
optimal w?

01:11:16.020 --> 01:11:19.360
The optimal w, if you use a
really high degree for your loss

01:11:19.360 --> 01:11:20.360
function.

01:11:20.900 --> 01:11:23.120
Remember, the loss function is
something that you pick in

01:11:23.120 --> 01:11:24.020
advance.

01:11:24.020 --> 01:11:26.600
If you use squared loss, the
optimal w is the mean.

01:11:26.600 --> 01:11:29.380
If you use absolute loss, the
optimal w is the median.

01:11:29.380 --> 01:11:33.160
I'm saying if you use degree 100
loss or degree a million loss,

01:11:33.160 --> 01:11:36.680
the optimal w is halfway between
the min and the max.

01:11:36.680 --> 01:11:39.620
What if there's like an infinite
amount of points on the left

01:11:39.620 --> 01:11:42.380
side and there's like One point
that's all the way on the right

01:11:42.380 --> 01:11:47.280
side That would still be true
for like L earlier for degree

01:11:47.280 --> 01:11:51.160
100 or a million loss It may
just take higher degrees to get

01:11:51.160 --> 01:11:53.410
to that convergence

01:11:55.500 --> 01:11:59.780
Obviously doesn't matter for
quadratic or to power 400 or

01:11:59.780 --> 01:12:03.120
whatever but does it ever matter
whether you're doing

01:12:03.120 --> 01:12:04.120
No,

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:07.280
because if you're doing an odd
number you're supposed to

01:12:07.280 --> 01:12:10.260
absolute value at first So if
you're doing like degree 3 or

01:12:10.260 --> 01:12:11.400
degree 5 loss,

01:12:11.400 --> 01:12:15.240
you'd take absolute value first
and then raise it to the

01:12:15.240 --> 01:12:18.580
exponent because if you have an
odd number,

01:12:18.580 --> 01:12:20.040
or sorry, you have an odd
degree,

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:22.080
a negative number to an odd
degree is still a negative

01:12:22.080 --> 01:12:24.040
number, which is why you'd
absolute it first.

01:12:24.040 --> 01:12:25.800
Yeah, good observation.

01:12:25.800 --> 01:12:26.420
OK.

01:12:26.420 --> 01:12:27.960
While we're here,

01:12:27.960 --> 01:12:30.780
so we talked about that sort of
infinity behavior, which is

01:12:30.780 --> 01:12:33.080
confusing.
Like I know it's hard to wrap

01:12:33.080 --> 01:12:35.120
your head around.
Here's another

01:12:35.120 --> 01:12:41.060
empirical risk function I want
you to think about.

01:13:00.240 --> 01:13:03.080
Just the loss function part on
the right

01:13:03.080 --> 01:13:05.480
is called zero-one loss.

01:13:05.480 --> 01:13:08.000
And as the name suggests, it can
only be equal to one of two

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:09.000
things,

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:12.460
one if the prediction is wrong,

01:13:12.460 --> 01:13:15.160
and zero if the prediction is
right.

01:13:15.160 --> 01:13:18.420
So if we're concerned with
minimizing average zero-one

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:19.420
loss,

01:13:20.460 --> 01:13:22.940
what do you think, and we're
using the constant model here,

01:13:22.940 --> 01:13:27.760
what do you think the optimal w
is?

01:13:27.760 --> 01:13:28.660
Brian.

01:13:28.660 --> 01:13:30.240
The mode.
How'd you get that?

01:13:30.900 --> 01:13:35.640
Just like when I have the most
amount directly on the point.

01:13:35.640 --> 01:13:36.640
Exactly.

01:13:37.820 --> 01:13:39.860
You can think of this loss
function as,

01:13:39.860 --> 01:13:42.460
again, it only has two possible
values, 1 or 0.

01:13:42.460 --> 01:13:45.600
And if the goal is to make the
average of the loss function as

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:46.840
small as possible,

01:13:46.840 --> 01:13:49.400
that is, you want this entire
expression to be as small as

01:13:49.400 --> 01:13:50.400
possible,

01:13:50.740 --> 01:13:53.380
you want to trigger this 0.
Think of this as like a function

01:13:53.380 --> 01:13:54.740
that's defined by an if
statement.

01:13:54.740 --> 01:13:57.740
If y equals w,

01:13:57.760 --> 01:14:00.300
return 0, otherwise return 1.

01:14:00.300 --> 01:14:03.720
So what you want to do is
trigger the 0 case as often as

01:14:03.720 --> 01:14:05.040
you can.

01:14:05.040 --> 01:14:06.400
And how do you do that?

01:14:06.400 --> 01:14:10.460
Well, you just pick w being
equal to the data point that was

01:14:10.460 --> 01:14:14.460
most common without regards to
the shape of the distribution.

01:14:14.980 --> 01:14:18.660
You just pick the one that's
most common.

01:14:18.660 --> 01:14:23.380
And so you pick the data point
that's the mode.

01:14:23.380 --> 01:14:25.940
Now, your question is, what if
there are multiple modes, or

01:14:25.940 --> 01:14:28.600
what if every data point appears
exactly once?

01:14:28.600 --> 01:14:31.400
Well then, there are multiple
minimizers for average 0, 1

01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:32.400
loss.

01:14:35.000 --> 01:14:37.480
My point in showing you this is
not that you need to sit and

01:14:37.480 --> 01:14:40.850
memorize lots of different loss
functions, but it's to show you

01:14:40.850 --> 01:14:43.900
that the loss function you
choose impacts the behavior of

01:14:43.900 --> 01:14:45.980
the resulting predictions.

01:14:45.980 --> 01:14:48.480
And things like the mean,
median, and mode, you've heard

01:14:48.480 --> 01:14:51.380
about before, but they're
actually related to these ideas

01:14:51.380 --> 01:14:54.900
of loss function, which I think
is interesting.

01:14:54.900 --> 01:14:57.900
Questions with anything we've
done so far?

01:14:58.560 --> 01:15:00.620
And I know we're moving quickly,
which is why,

01:15:00.620 --> 01:15:03.480
even on the announcement slide
today, I said make sure to spend

01:15:03.480 --> 01:15:07.230
some time reading through the
notes carefully.

01:15:07.860 --> 01:15:08.420
Alex?

01:15:08.420 --> 01:15:12.080
If each data point only appeared
once, would it even matter what

01:15:12.080 --> 01:15:14.120
you choose for w for like the
minimum?

01:15:14.120 --> 01:15:14.440
No,

01:15:14.440 --> 01:15:15.520
it wouldn't.
They'd all have the same

01:15:15.520 --> 01:15:16.520
minimizing

01:15:16.740 --> 01:15:19.740
value of empirical risk.

01:15:21.020 --> 01:15:23.620
To be clear, just because you
can use a loss function doesn't

01:15:23.620 --> 01:15:24.440
mean you should.

01:15:24.440 --> 01:15:26.400
What is important is to
understand how different loss

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:27.900
functions behave.

01:15:28.380 --> 01:15:29.880
Sound reasonable?

01:15:29.880 --> 01:15:33.620
What I want to give you now is
like a five minute preview of

01:15:33.620 --> 01:15:38.120
the next thing we're going to
study.

01:15:38.240 --> 01:15:40.740
You're going to play around with
this, especially in code,

01:15:40.740 --> 01:15:43.180
including using scikit-learn,
which is like the industry

01:15:43.180 --> 01:15:48.560
standard Python package for
non-neural network machine

01:15:48.560 --> 01:15:49.680
learning.
You're going to see this in lab

01:15:49.680 --> 01:15:50.680
tomorrow.

01:15:51.280 --> 01:15:53.280
I want to give you a preview of
what that's going to look like.

01:15:53.280 --> 01:15:55.280
And then on Thursday, we will
spend all our time discussing

01:15:55.280 --> 01:15:57.240
the theory of it.

01:15:57.240 --> 01:15:59.780
Remind me, what was the name of
the second model we said we

01:15:59.780 --> 01:16:02.260
would study? We've sort of
talked in depth about the

01:16:02.260 --> 01:16:03.760
constant model.
What was the next thing?

01:16:07.180 --> 01:16:07.640
Paul?

01:16:07.640 --> 01:16:08.460
Linear?

01:16:08.460 --> 01:16:10.600
Yeah, the simple linear
regression model.

01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:13.840
And simple just means it uses
one input variable or one

01:16:13.840 --> 01:16:14.840
feature.

01:16:14.940 --> 01:16:17.560
Later, we will learn about
multiple linear regression.

01:16:17.560 --> 01:16:21.620
and that's when we use multiple
input variables.

01:16:21.620 --> 01:16:25.370
So I'll say next model,

01:16:26.180 --> 01:16:31.360
simple linear regression.

01:16:33.180 --> 01:16:34.420
And as you can see,

01:16:34.420 --> 01:16:37.780
and simple linear regression is
really just a rebranded y equals

01:16:37.780 --> 01:16:40.860
mx plus b.
It's just that the slope now is

01:16:40.860 --> 01:16:43.880
called w1, and the intercept is
now called w0.

01:16:43.880 --> 01:16:49.200
And what I've shown you here are
three different possible

01:16:49.200 --> 01:16:52.950
choices of intercepts and slopes
for this data set.

01:16:53.120 --> 01:16:54.340
And the question is,

01:16:54.340 --> 01:16:57.520
what is the most optimal
intercept and slope?

01:16:57.520 --> 01:17:00.620
One thing you'll notice is that
the way in which we are making

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:02.120
our predictions,

01:17:02.660 --> 01:17:06.580
now it includes the x's
themselves.

01:17:06.780 --> 01:17:09.400
So for different times we leave
in the morning, we're getting

01:17:09.400 --> 01:17:10.900
different predictions.

01:17:10.980 --> 01:17:13.060
So with the constant model,

01:17:13.060 --> 01:17:15.320
the parameter was the
prediction.

01:17:15.320 --> 01:17:18.100
Like w star equals 50 means
we're just predicting 50 all the

01:17:18.100 --> 01:17:18.980
time.

01:17:18.980 --> 01:17:22.120
But here, finding that the slope
is equal to 100 or something

01:17:22.120 --> 01:17:24.020
doesn't mean you're predicting
100.

01:17:24.020 --> 01:17:27.160
It just means that's one of the
pieces of the formula you are

01:17:27.160 --> 01:17:29.240
using to make your predictions.

01:17:29.240 --> 01:17:31.800
And instead of finding just one
optimal parameter,

01:17:31.800 --> 01:17:35.500
now we need to find an optimal
combination of parameters.

01:17:35.500 --> 01:17:40.200
So what does the math for that
look like?

01:17:40.200 --> 01:17:43.060
We're going to look at our
three-step recipe for empirical

01:17:43.060 --> 01:17:45.310
risk minimization again.

01:17:47.740 --> 01:17:53.140
The model we'll choose is the
simple linear regression

01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:54.140
model.

01:18:02.580 --> 01:18:05.180
We'll stick with squared loss,

01:18:05.180 --> 01:18:10.540
though in the next homework you
will look at simple linear

01:18:10.540 --> 01:18:13.540
regression and absolute loss.

01:18:13.740 --> 01:18:16.700
And so what does the average
loss look like?

01:18:16.700 --> 01:18:17.240
Well,

01:18:17.240 --> 01:18:20.600
it should have a 1 over n and a
sum across all of our data

01:18:20.600 --> 01:18:21.600
points.

01:18:22.060 --> 01:18:25.810
Every average loss will have
this.

01:18:25.920 --> 01:18:29.200
I'm going to have yi minus
something squared.

01:18:29.200 --> 01:18:32.200
What is that something?

01:18:36.400 --> 01:18:39.400
W0 plus w1 xi.

01:18:42.060 --> 01:18:44.740
W0 plus w1 xi.

01:18:44.740 --> 01:18:48.020
Great, and that difference is
being squared.

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:51.900
So I might still call this RSQ,

01:18:51.900 --> 01:18:56.400
but this function now has how
many input variables?

01:18:57.300 --> 01:19:01.050
Two, because there are two
parameters.

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:07.440
And so what we will look at next
class is how you find the

01:19:07.440 --> 01:19:10.380
optimal combination of
parameters that minimizes this

01:19:10.380 --> 01:19:11.360
function.

01:19:11.360 --> 01:19:14.500
But to do that, we will need to
introduce basic ideas from

01:19:14.500 --> 01:19:16.000
multivariable calculus,

01:19:16.340 --> 01:19:17.020
calc 3,

01:19:17.020 --> 01:19:22.000
because we now don't just have a
single variable along which we

01:19:22.000 --> 01:19:24.140
take a derivative.
We have two variables and so we

01:19:24.140 --> 01:19:27.360
have to take what are called
partial derivatives.

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:29.640
And just so you have it written
down,

01:19:29.640 --> 01:19:35.060
I'll give you some of the
optimal

01:19:35.060 --> 01:19:36.060
formulas.

01:19:38.460 --> 01:19:40.560
But right away you're seeing
that this is a little different

01:19:40.560 --> 01:19:43.920
than before because an x appears
in the formula,

01:19:43.920 --> 01:19:46.280
which we should expect it to,
right? And it would be weird if

01:19:46.280 --> 01:19:49.920
the formula for the optimal
slope

01:19:49.920 --> 01:19:53.760
didn't involve the x-axis
component.

01:20:02.980 --> 01:20:06.760
So this is the optimal slope,

01:20:06.760 --> 01:20:12.440
w1 star and w0 star.
You see it is defined in terms

01:20:12.440 --> 01:20:14.460
of the optimal slope.
You find the optimal slope

01:20:14.460 --> 01:20:17.620
first, and then use it to find
the optimal intercept.

01:20:17.620 --> 01:20:19.460
And the last thing I'll see
here,

01:20:19.460 --> 01:20:21.480
and you don't really need to
rush to write this down because

01:20:21.480 --> 01:20:23.080
it's all in the notes, of
course.

01:20:23.080 --> 01:20:26.180
But you may want to refer to it
in tomorrow's lab.

01:20:26.180 --> 01:20:30.680
For the optimal slope, there are
lots of equivalent

01:20:31.760 --> 01:20:32.760
formulas.

01:20:34.060 --> 01:20:37.020
So this is just one formula for
the optimal slope, but I will

01:20:37.020 --> 01:20:40.200
show you a few others when we
come back on Thursday.

01:20:40.200 --> 01:20:42.400
And when you're working on
homework problems, is the one

01:20:42.400 --> 01:20:45.620
that you use depends on what the
